# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gmail-multi-mcp — OAuth credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the two values below:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored. You can also skip this file entirely and let
# `npm run setup` ask for the credentials and store them in
# ~/.gmail-multi-mcp/config.json. If both exist, the environment wins.
#
# A `.env` in the project root is loaded automatically at startup via Node's
# built-in `process.loadEnvFile()` — no dotenv dependency. That function
# requires **Node >= 20.12**; on an older Node this file is ignored in silence,
# which looks exactly like "no credentials configured".
#
# Anything already exported in your shell takes precedence over this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where these come from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. Create a project:  https://console.cloud.google.com/
#   2. Enable the Gmail API for it:
#        https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/gmail.googleapis.com
#   3. Configure the OAuth consent screen (Google Auth Platform), audience
#      "External", and add every Gmail address you plan to connect under
#      "Test users" — an account not listed there cannot grant consent.
#   4. Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID
#        Application type: **Desktop app**   <- this exact type
#   5. Copy the client ID and client secret into the two variables below.
#
# ONE client serves EVERY Gmail account you add. That is the whole point: you
# do this once, then `npm run setup` as many times as you have mailboxes.

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-your-client-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no redirect URI here, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The setup flow opens a throwaway HTTP server on a random free loopback port
# and hands that URL to Google. Desktop-app clients accept any loopback port,
# so nothing has to be registered in advance.
#
# The callback uses http://127.0.0.1:<port>, NOT http://localhost:<port>. They
# are the same machine but not the same cookie origin: every dev server you
# have ever run on a localhost port can leave cookies there, and the browser
# would send all of them to the callback — overflowing the header limit and
# failing with HTTP 431 *after* consent was already granted.
